    "['family inet [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'router bgp 100', 'parent_command': ['router bgp 100'], 'root': 'router bgp 100', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper BGP family configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp 100\\n neighbor 10.2.1.2 activate\\n##\\n\\nNotes:\\n1. In Cisco, the BGP process is initiated with \"router bgp [local-as]\" (we used 100 from the peer-as in the Juniper config)\\n2. The \"family inet unicast\" in Juniper roughly translates to simply activating the neighbor in Cisco, as IPv4 unicast is the default address family\\n3. The neighbor IP matches the one specified in the Juniper configuration (10.2.1.2)\\n4. Unlike Juniper which explicitly defines address families, Cisco activates IPv4 unicast by default when you activate a neighbor', 'trans_template': 'router bgp 100'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'neighbor 10.2.1.2 activate', 'parent_command': ['router bgp 100'], 'root': 'router bgp 100', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper BGP family configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp 100\\n neighbor 10.2.1.2 activate\\n##\\n\\nNotes:\\n1. In Cisco, the BGP process is initiated with \"router bgp [local-as]\" (we used 100 from the peer-as in the Juniper config)\\n2. The \"family inet unicast\" in Juniper roughly translates to simply activating the neighbor in Cisco, as IPv4 unicast is the default address family\\n3. The neighbor IP matches the one specified in the Juniper configuration (10.2.1.2)\\n4. Unlike Juniper which explicitly defines address families, Cisco activates IPv4 unicast by default when you activate a neighbor', 'trans_template': 'neighbor 10.2.1.2 activate'}]]": 1,
