he equivalent Cisco configuration for the given Juniper policy statement:\\n\\n##\\nroute-map bgp-export permit 10\\n match ip address prefix-list BGP-FILTER\\n!\\nip prefix-list BGP-FILTER permit 10.2.1.0/24\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. Juniper\\'s \"policy-statement\" translates to Cisco\\'s \"route-map\"\\n2. \"term 1\" becomes the sequence number (10 in this case)\\n3. The exact route filter is implemented using a prefix-list in Cisco\\n4. The \"then accept\" action is implied by the \"permit\" keyword in the route-map\\n5. Cisco requires separate prefix-list or access-list definitions for matching, unlike Juniper which can match directly in the policy', 'trans_template': '!'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'ip prefix-list BGP-FILTER permit 10.2.1.0/24', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'ip prefix-list BGP-FILTER permit 10.2.1.0/24', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the equivalent Cisco configuration for the given Juniper policy statement:\\n\\n##\\nroute-map bgp-export permit 10\\n match ip address prefix-list BGP-FILTER\\n!\\nip prefix-list BGP-FILTER permit 10.2.1.0/24\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. Juniper\\'s \"policy-statement\" translates to Cisco\\'s \"route-map\"\\n2. \"term 1\" becomes the sequence number (10 in this case)\\n3. The exact route filter is implemented using a prefix-list in Cisco\\n4. The \"then accept\" action is implied by the \"permit\" keyword in the route-map\\n5. Cisco requires separate prefix-list or access-list definitions for matching, unlike Juniper which can match directly in the policy', 'trans_template': 'ip prefix-list BGP-FILTER permit 10.2.1.0/24'}]]": 1,
